  "query_preview": "..\nToday through Tuesday...\n\nSeasonable conditions will exist today with high temperatures in \nthe 50s to lower 60s (F). While much less windy than yesterday, \nbreezy westerly winds are expected in spots today with peak wind \ngusts mainly in the teens and 20s mph, though a few gusts into the\n30s mph are forecast over the foothills west of Billings. While \nmostly dry conditions will prevail, a passing shortwave trough \nwill bring a few snow showers to the mountains, mainly the \nAbsaroka- Beartooth Mountains, early this morning. There is also a\nslight chance (15 percent) of a few light rain/snow showers in \nfar southeastern Montana (mainly southern portions of Carter \nCounty) this morning, but recent model runs have trended a bit \nfarther south with this precipitation. Any precipitation that \noccurs today will be light, with a dusting to an inch or two of \nsnow possible over the Absaroka-Beartooth Mountains. \n\nThursday into Friday will remain mainly dry with seasonable \ntemperatures as we sit between a developing low pressure system \nover the west coast of the United States and a ridge of high \npressure over the central United States.\n\nLate Friday into Saturday, the chance of precipitation begins to \nincrease as the area of low pressure over the west coast of the \nUnited States starts to approach our region. With this, \natmospheric moisture values will increase and occasional waves of \nenergy should be enough for at least isolated showers at times, \nmainly over the mountains and foothills. \n\nSunday into Monday, the area of low pressure looks to cross \nthrough our region in some fashion, however, there is still a good\namount of uncertainty in how exactly this will occur. While the \nplacement of the low pressure center will dictate what kind of \nimpacts we see, there is at least the chance of seeing cooler \ntemperatures, precipitation, and windy conditions returning to \nmuch of the area. Behind this weather system, ensembles suggest a \nperiod of slightly warmer and drier conditions may return around \nTuesday with a ridge of high pressure moving in aloft. LA",
